#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073434 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/id #uuid "2ce03c34-8e2c-5554-a129-1eaf05ad46fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12548" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $x=5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/95/952be9b27e5350f755f0f534883f3c70516bb4e03b34e9448ab5d43950d4bad6.png)\n\nSince the line $x = 5$ is vertical, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(3,2)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $5.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd = |3 - 5| = 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073435 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/id #uuid "64a6782b-aa64-5027-8311-3498c19c39e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12549" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/5c/5ccfa3c27c6bb2759ffc37c73b3640583fa962ee79f47b21f02ac0c1d9164b00.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x - 5$ and passes through the point $(1,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{1}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-2.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-1) \\\\ y-3 & =-2x+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{1}{2}x-5 \\\\ y &=-2x+5\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by setting the two equations equal and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x-5 & =-2x+5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+2x & =5+5 \\\\ \\frac{5}{2}x & =10 \\\\ x & =10\\cdot \\frac{2}{5} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-3.$ So, our solution is $x=4$ and $y=-3.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $\\left( 4,-3\\right).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(1,3)$ and $\\left( 4, -3 \\right)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(4-1)}^{2}+{(-3-3)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9}\\sqrt{5} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(1,3)$ and the line $y=\\dfrac{1}{2}x -5$ is $3 \\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "57fc631b-c251-55d2-a9d5-6b41fc4bb00c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12612" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c48fc163b3c9543dcfdd03b20882f0d2a19747672c6b1db2a42a683f0014fe25.png)\n\n \n\nFind the point on the line $y-x=4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-x & =4 \\\\ y & =x+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y =  x + 4$ and passes through the point $(3,3).$ \n\nThe given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-3 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=x+4 \\\\ y &=-x+6\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =-x+6 \\\\ x+x & =6-4 \\\\ 2x & =2 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=5.$ So, our solution is $x=1$ and $y=5.$ Consequently, the lines intersect at the point $(1,5).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y-x= 4$ that is closest to the point $(3,3)$ is  $(1,5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91964766-092f-5fad-99d5-a15bc5d93379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540773} {:answer/id #uuid "95f90cf7-7763-5c6f-9b1d-0fd3a0e23ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540777} {:answer/id #uuid "a38f5ac6-c994-5a87-8126-2a4823f37432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540801} {:answer/id #uuid "b52b2dcd-c345-543d-9c13-8bddd315750b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540829} {:answer/id #uuid "d595c3ee-db9c-5f39-9662-0ad2912a3dc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95f90cf7-7763-5c6f-9b1d-0fd3a0e23ad8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540777} :answer-field/id #uuid "7072da0e-d425-5fcf-b3a1-d1e9e664e903" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540986}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52deb492-2c28-55be-ad12-3e097a96cc24" :question/math-academy-id "q-114324" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(4,-1)$ and the line $x=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/ae/ae9c7b44a3e246fb6e0e9f90c1288ef42814c22d705c944e46fe0eb890a5a998.png)\n\nSince the line $x=-2$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(4,-1)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between the $x$-coordinate of the point and $-2.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|4-(-2)|=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3be87dbe-29dc-5043-9a79-a86b2fd5a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540628} {:answer/id #uuid "4c39f4de-1a6a-52cf-8624-125d851fe285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540664} {:answer/id #uuid "635a77b6-fd8f-5cf2-8d7f-1598dc609b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540696} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1c0892-2c18-5576-a828-53b1cfdefbaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540717} {:answer/id #uuid "781f5f51-a2bb-5ef5-9600-e203136f5055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3be87dbe-29dc-5043-9a79-a86b2fd5a880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540628} :answer-field/id #uuid "1932c306-3c23-58d3-a967-76e055fcd6ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "777e8b7e-3aad-5700-9be0-3920699b2b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-114342" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(8,1)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/52/52bfc27007c57351cdf90d88d444676266edc5d2704314db0d99fd9dca8e367f.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2$ and passes through the point $(8,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-8) \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-12+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2}x-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3}x+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-11 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{3}{2}x & =-11-2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{6}x & =-13 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=6$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=6$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(6,-2).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(8,1)$ and $(6,-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6-8)^{2}+(-2-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(8,1)$ and the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x+2$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074513 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0498568d-b327-5f78-89e8-8587c0368b6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339162} {:answer/id #uuid "850104ea-f24f-5eb3-bc01-1f1701a3ca6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340323} {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} {:answer/id #uuid "c8516292-870e-5d6a-9fc2-73422fa2795d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340902} {:answer/id #uuid "d93f1851-2a54-53fb-9fee-5fb53f3f5e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a884e107-b09a-542c-94ff-f2323802311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1068806-46ea-595d-a611-e82e67e43211" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2a5b92a-6d2c-561b-999b-9dedaf72311f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74060" :question/problem "![](images/f9/f97972b1765e58a76475a99a97e4a8818b05efa1f26a79a232b250b6ef681e9b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the equation of the given line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3x & =3 \\\\ 2y & =3x+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}$ and passes through the point $(2,-2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =-\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{6}{13}\\cdot \\frac{13}{6} \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into either of the two equations gives $y=0.$ So, our solution is $x=-1$ and $y=0.$ So, the lines intersect at the point $(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $2y-3x=3$ that is closest to the point $(2,-2)$ is  $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} {:answer/id #uuid "7d839962-7657-5bb7-904b-97d127c09ca6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{9}{2},1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340259} {:answer/id #uuid "a38a60c9-c99e-5333-a142-a09672caa6e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340611} {:answer/id #uuid "befaa2f8-684a-5c95-bb90-4e92f8bb8e1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340830} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc6bc70-eb00-5071-8312-8709fa64cf86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6304ff82-aa6c-50b7-b79e-a11e972b7aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340009} :answer-field/id #uuid "f952c9dc-1256-5a8a-9688-3f9bfd070213" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "853cfa3c-da08-562e-b7e9-24b69cddbfcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-74061" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d478fb4499ee19a7c385ae93dd5ad790404351ceda63c20341b9996a1466ca60.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ and passes through the point $(2,1).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $-\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{3}{2}x-3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=\\frac{3}{2}x-2 \\\\ y &=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}x-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x & =-\\frac{2}{3}x \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}x+\\frac{2}{3}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0$ into either of the two equations gives $y=-2.$ So, our solution is $x=0$ and $y=-2.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(0,-2).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=-\\frac{2}{3}x-2$ that is closest to the point $(2,1)$ is $(0,-2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342181 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073810 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "522b66f4-8a46-5dd0-a9d7-3b081c0ed157" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Horizontal or Vertical Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} {:answer/id #uuid "836328cc-8806-5c45-bf00-1c3923682d40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340310} {:answer/id #uuid "9909d2b8-e7fa-56b5-9269-e5951dc53bcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340509} {:answer/id #uuid "a86ba75a-1b29-5797-9053-bf2a3ad7aa27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340658} {:answer/id #uuid "b0de169a-6a53-5b29-85e4-09cc91632445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59078a96-a4c1-5355-9bdf-3b0f39c93291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339938} :answer-field/id #uuid "cab68815-1850-5693-b986-be3ac48add05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79f2f5ac-44c5-5b82-9c80-2524e0bd699c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74202" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(-1,-4)$ and the line $x=4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the given situation.\n\n![](images/d4/d46a6ed76af5c226e327ba197fe019df0d9085e095ca4781898aba54268a1706.png)\n\nSince the line $x=4$ is a vertical line, the shortest distance between this line and the point $(-1,-4)$ is given by the absolute value of the difference between $x$-coordinate of the point and $4.$ \n\nSo, the shortest distance is\n\n$$\nd=|-1-4|=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7030a12-bd18-5f8b-84eb-28b6f216f972" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between a Point and a Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d2f5dd4-e22e-536b-8ce7-2a9b4757513e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540577} {:answer/id #uuid "706ad2cc-287a-563f-aecf-c4e029c86155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540723} {:answer/id #uuid "8ff278d4-c3af-52d0-a261-2ce96bee14a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540768} {:answer/id #uuid "afc25060-6cdc-5f7a-b06c-abd717564cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540822} {:answer/id #uuid "d25821c3-b2f1-5f34-b7c8-4715d5113d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afc25060-6cdc-5f7a-b06c-abd717564cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540822} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9538eb7-9f5e-5704-8c29-873db219be68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3115c643-189e-5d0d-96c2-b1b8ae304036" :question/math-academy-id "q-74419" :question/problem "Find the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $y=x+1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram describing our situation. \n\n![](images/80/80f3e7d79f84a3894e563c78747e22ccf11c889783c39ed6fb688a4242160d60.png)\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=x+1$ and passes through the point $(3,2).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $1,$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-1.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-1(x-3) \\\\ y-2 & =-x+3 \\\\ y & =-x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=x+1 \\\\ y=-x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =-x+5 \\\\ x+x & =-1+5 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ into either of the two equations gives $y=3.$ So, our solution is $x=2$ and $y=3.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(2,3).$\n\nFinally, we find the distance $d$ between the points $(3,2)$ and $(2,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2-3)^{2}+(3-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the shortest distance between the point $(3,2)$ and the line $y=x+1$ is $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541092 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "59d3a391-c163-5a04-944c-5d61a3950e03" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Line That Is Closest to a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f7cf30-3ed7-5793-9002-3529283cccb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540587} {:answer/id #uuid "937d33d5-a8a7-5da9-bb11-1ede9ac95d6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540775} {:answer/id #uuid "b544aa9a-5b87-55cf-b2e4-1e042d4dc7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540830} {:answer/id #uuid "e847e91b-a66b-5b3c-a257-23c792bc4fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{4}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540922} {:answer/id #uuid "e8f4f8f6-66d2-56a3-a1e0-b35ad6aa0fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8f4f8f6-66d2-56a3-a1e0-b35ad6aa0fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540923} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5a7caf1-f1b3-50b7-8338-9e475499f496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "683e7ef8-4750-58e2-a136-8e19dd7482de" :question/math-academy-id "q-74423" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc1432b521a372c9a7e264700cc58eef96c34b347e0300f5b7c323066ed0cc7b.png)\n\nFind the point on the line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ that is closest to the point $(-1,7).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the equation of the line that's perpendicular to $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ and passes through the point $(-1,7).$\n\n The given line has a slope of $\\frac{4}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the perpendicular line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-7 & =-\\frac{3}{4}(x+1) \\\\ y-7 & =-\\frac{3}{4}x-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now find the point where the two lines intersect. To do this, we solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{4}{3}x \\\\ y=-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this system by putting the two equations equal to one another and solving for $x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3}x & =-\\frac{3}{4}x+\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x+\\frac{3}{4}x & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ \\frac{25}{12}x & =\\frac{25}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot \\frac{12}{25} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3$ into either of the two equations gives $y=4.$ So, our solution is $x=3$ and $y=4.$ Therefore, the lines intersect at the point $(3,4).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the point on the line $y=\\frac{4}{3}x$ that is closest to the point $(-1,7)$ is $(3,4).$"}]}
